First: I don't consider explore mode to be quite the same thing.  I
would be very surprised if more than a few new Nethack players entered
explore mode on their own.  Regardless, there's an important difference
between "same game with voluntary death" and "baby version of the
original game."  I've never seen explore mode as an option - I *like*
there to be SOME level of risk; what I'm talking about here is toning
that level of risk down to some extent.

As far as I'm concerned, risk and negative consequences are an
incredibly important part of the game.  I don't think they should be
completely eliminated by a very, very long stretch.

I also agree that the learning experiences are very important.  That is
definitely one of the biggest reasons I keep playing - I keeping
figuring new stuff out, I keep finding new solutions, and I keep
finding new ways to adapt my choices to the way I want to play the
game.

On the other hand, the risks as they exist currently are minimally
scaled.  I think there are many ways to teach new players about the
intricacies and experiences that are vital to the game - including
killing them at times.  What I am talking about is mostly *increasing*
the chances for a positive outcome at early stages of the game and,
likewise, *decreasing* the chances for a negative outcome at early
stages of the game, combined with allowing self-selecting "baby"
players to choose characters that are weighted differently.  As far at
that last statement goes, I think some very tiny changes to some of the
existing NH & SE classes would go a very long way - for example, taking
the Ice/Flame mage roles and offering them one or two (added) inventory
slots that would get randomly selected spellbooks/wands/rings, a la the
wizard role.  I cannot tell you how much of a difference that startup
spellbook of identify, charm monster, or wand of polymorph has made to
me when it's happened.  When that DOES happen, I immediately invest
much more in the character than I would have otherwise.  Jacking up the
amount baby characters could carry would also be a huge help.

As for going from baby NH to "grown-up" NH, personally? I probably
never would.  As mentioned, I cheat in order to be able to achieve
those goals.  If I could do that from within the game, I absolutely
would.  That said, I think that risks can, in fact, be scaled up much
more so than they are now.  Right now risk scaling is primarily in
monster level.  AFAIK, there is no difference in the chance of, for
example, getting a water demon on level 1 versus level 41.  Upping the
probability of good loot generation in the first few DLs would help
tremendously.  Building in more explicit education would also be of
great help: I'd played the game for a few years, before I was really
online as a matter of course, always assuming that the order of the
dungeons was prescriptive, before I read (here, I believe) that I
should simply not do the Mines until XP level 10 or above.  That little
fact helped me a lot - I'd always just figured that if the entrance to
the Mines was on level 2, I should do it at level 2.  Obviously the
internet helped me and plenty others figure out what I should do, but
not everyone knows to look for the kinds of resources there are.  And I
still die on a highly routine basis even knowing everything I know.  I
just gave my boyfriend a lengthy lecture on where to stand in shops,
piling all the stuff you don't want on a cursed item, training the pet
to shoplift, what kinds of armor will show up as things like "old
gloves" and "opera cloaks," and that his mace and robe are probably
cursed because he insists on quaffing from fountains, and I still can't
survive for longer than a few levels.

The upshot is that I don't think risk should be dumped, I don't think
explore mode is close to what I'm talking about, and I agree that death
can be educational.  I do think that the level of risk can be far more
gracefully scaled than it is now, particularly given the number of
calculations that are performed practically every turn; say you made
more of the positive effect chance generators 1 in (<level>+1) instead
of 1 in <number> up to a certain point - that alone would make things
easier without requiring a whole lot of work.

The thing is that risk becomes a negative when it is too great by a.,
preventing people from playing the game (most of the people I know who
I've recommended NH to have quit after being killed by things like
kittens seven minutes (if that) into playing), and b., preventing
learning by instilling fear.  This happens when the level of risk is
too great, not when the level of risk is appropriate.  If the level of
risk is appropriate, it should *encourage* play by other people than
those who are either masochists or who respond purely to the "I'm gonna
beat this if it's the last thing I do" kind of challenge, and it should
*encourage* exploration and learning by making the risks and rewards
equable in learning situations.

Certainly many of the risks can be mitigated, but only if you know
exactly what you're doing (or are way luckier than a blessed
luckstone's gonna get ya) - and often not even then.  A new player is
unlikely to know what happened to begin with, let alone how to fix it.
I think that teaching them the answers to those questions makes the
game what it is, but I also think that they ain't gonna stick around to
learn the answers to those questions if the lessons are too hard.

